#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[Lattices, theta series, and periods]

== The dual lattice in a familiar theta series <c05s01>

Let $P$ be a full lattice in Euclidean $ℝ^d$ and $P^*$ its dual under the scalar product. The theta series $Theta_(P)(t)=sum_(v in P)exp(-pi t ||v||^2)$ records its vector lengths. The same Fourier argument as for the one-dimensional Gaussian gives
$ Theta_(P)(t)=frac(t^(-frac(d,2)),vol(ℝ^d slash P)) Theta_(P^*)(frac(1,t)). $
For $P=2ℤ$, this says that summing the Gaussian over even integers is equivalent to summing a rescaled Gaussian over half-integers and dividing by two. The factor of two is the covolume of $2ℤ$. Dropping it changes the transformation law. In an adelic theta integral there are many such factors, one for each change of local lattice and measure.

The integral structure matters even when the vector space and its real norm stay the same. The sublattice generated by $(p,0),(1,p)$ in $ℤ^2$ has determinant $p^2$, hence index $p^2$. Over $ℤ_p$ its quotient is cyclic of length two because an entry of the matrix is a unit. Over $ℤ_ℓ$ for $ℓ!=p$ the matrix is invertible. Its real covolume is $p^2$ times that of $ℤ^2$. The finite lengths and the real covolume describe the same index. Integral theta lifts turn this elementary comparison into an inequality with controlled losses.

== Recovering a lattice from its basis and its characters <c05s02>

Write a full lattice in Euclidean space as $P=B ℤ^d$ for a real invertible $d$ by $d$ matrix $B$. Replacing its basis multiplies $B$ on the right by a unimodular integer matrix, so $|det B|$ is independent of the basis. It equals the volume of the half-open parallelepiped $B[0,1)^d$, a measurable fundamental domain for translations by $P$. A linear character $x mapsto exp(2pi i chevron.l x,y chevron.r)$ descends to the quotient $ℝ^d slash P$ exactly when its value is one on $P$. This condition is $B^t y in ℤ^d$, so the dual lattice is $P^*=B^(-t)ℤ^d$. In particular the two covolumes are reciprocal.

For a rapidly decreasing smooth function $f$, periodize $f(x+v)$ over $v in P$. Integrating against a character of the quotient gives its Fourier coefficient $frac(hat(f)(y),abs(det B))$ for $y in P^*$. To compute the coefficient, translate each integral from the original fundamental parallelepiped to its translate and join the resulting domains. The phases match precisely because $y$ belongs to the dual. For Schwartz $f$ both sides converge absolutely. Evaluating the Fourier expansion at a shift $h$ yields
$ sum_(v in P) f(v+h)=frac(1,|det B|) sum_(y in P^*) hat(f)(y)e^(2pi i chevron.l h,y chevron.r). $
Taking a Gaussian gives the theta transformation from the start of the chapter. The same identity now records *which coset* of $P$ one is summing over. Translation of the lattice has become multiplication by a unit phase. This is the real counterpart of the finite additive transform in Chapter 4.

#example[A parity condition in one dimension][Take $P=2ℤ$ with shifts $0$ and $1$. Their transform phases at $y=frac(k,2)$ are $1$ and $(-1)^k$. Adding the two theta series kills odd $k$ on the dual side and recovers the Gaussian sum over all integers. Subtracting them retains only odd $k$, with a factor of two canceling the covolume two. The alternating sum over integers then equals a positive Gaussian sum over half-integer frequencies after scaling. The coset difference has introduced a condition on the dual variable that could not be recovered from the unshifted theta series alone.]

The same computation handles a nonstandard metric. If $A$ is a real symmetric positive definite matrix, take $f(x)=exp(-pi t x^t A x)$. Diagonalizing $A$ orthogonally and changing each coordinate gives
$ hat(f)(y)=frac(t^(-frac(d,2)),sqrt(det A)) exp(-frac(pi y^t A^(-1)y,t)). $
The real Gram matrix of the lattice basis is $B^t A B$. Its volume in that metric is $|det B|sqrt(det A)$. Altering a real metric changes this archimedean factor even if the set of integral vectors is fixed. A formula which compares two integral spaces with different quotient metrics must account for that change along with finite indices.

== How much of the lattice does a determinant see? <c05s03>

Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $varpi$ and valuation normalized by $v(varpi)=1$. Suppose an integral matrix describes an injective endomorphism of $R^d$ of full rational rank. Row and column operations over $R$ reduce the matrix to diagonal entries $varpi^(a_1),dots,varpi^(a_d)$ with $0<=a_1<=dots<=a_d$. To see existence, pick an entry of least valuation and move it to the first diagonal position. It divides all entries, so integral row and column operations clear the remainder of its row and column. Every entry of the smaller block remains divisible by the pivot. Repeat the procedure and remove units from the diagonal. This gives the elementary divisors of the map.

The quotient then has a direct sum decomposition $Q≃⨁_i R slash varpi^(a_i)R$. Its length, the number of residue-field factors in a composition series, is $sum_i a_i=v(det B)$. The elementary divisors contain more information than this one number. For $r>=1$,
$ D_r=len(Q slash varpi^r Q)=sum_i min(r,a_i), $
$ D_r-D_(r-1)=|{i : a_i>=r}|. $
These differences recover the individual exponents and hence their uniqueness. They also explain a danger in using just the rational rank and the determinant. Neither determines how many independent directions survive at a given power of the uniformizer.

#example[Two ways for an index to have valuation two][Over $ℤ_p$ compare $B=mat(p,1;0,p)$ and $C=mat(p,0;0,p)$. Both have determinant $p^2$. The unit entry of $B$ gives exponents $0,2$, while those of $C$ are $1,1$. For $B$ the successive $D_r$ begin $1,2,2$. For $C$ they begin $2,2,2$. Reduction modulo $p$ retains rank one of the first image and rank zero of the second. Two congruence conditions applied in the same direction can give the same index as one condition in each of two independent directions.]

The description has a rectangular version used implicitly whenever several images enter a common target. Suppose $B$ has $r$ independent columns in $R^d$. Its image $L$ spans an $r$-dimensional vector subspace over the fraction field. Intersect that subspace with $R^d$ to obtain its saturation $L^("sat")$. The finite quotient $L^("sat") slash L$ measures failure of the original image to be primitive. In adapted bases the nonzero diagonal entries give its elementary-divisor exponents. Their sum is the minimum of the valuations of the nonzero $r$ by $r$ minors. Indeed unimodular row and column operations preserve the ideal generated by the maximal minors, by the formula for minors of a product. On the diagonal matrix this ideal is generated by the product of the nonzero elementary divisors.

Over the integers the corresponding index has one local length at each prime. For a full-rank square integral matrix of determinant $D!=0$, only primes dividing $D$ have positive length, and
$ log|D|=sum_p len_(ℤ_p)(op("coker")(B) ⊗ ℤ_p) log p. $
At the real place $|D|$ is the covolume of the column lattice relative to the standard lattice. Thus an archimedean magnitude is exactly the sum of weighted local lengths. This equality is the elementary form of the determinant bookkeeping used for adelic vector spaces.

== A determinant inequality with explicit normalizations <c05s04>

Let $v_1,dots,v_r$ be independent vectors in Euclidean space and $G=(chevron.l v_i,v_j chevron.r)$ their Gram matrix. Orthogonally project $v_i$ off the span of its predecessors, and call the residual vector $v_i^perp$. Elementary column operations turn the parallelepiped into an orthogonal one of the same volume. Consequently
$ det G=product_(i=1)^r ||v_i^perp||^2<=product_(i=1)^r ||v_i||^2. $
For a full square matrix of integer columns this is Hadamard's inequality. Combining it with local divisibility gives
$ sum_p v_(p)(det B)log p<=sum_(i=1)^d log||v_i||. $
If a construction forces the left side to be large while supplying independent columns with controlled norms, the two bounds can become incompatible. If the columns become dependent the determinant vanishes and the logarithmic identity can no longer be applied to them. This is why lower bounds for the rank of a family of images intervene in a proof using this principle.

For example consider $v_1=(M,1)$ and $v_2=(M+1,1)$ with $M$ a large integer. Each length is of order $M$, yet the absolute determinant is one. Their angle is sufficiently small to make the covolume constant. By contrast the columns $(p^a,0),(0,1)$ have a large local length $a$ at $p$ and are perpendicular, so the product of lengths exactly accounts for their determinant. Real size in individual columns, local divisibility in individual entries, and independence of their images contribute distinct inputs.

This comparison is invariant when one rescales a rational map and counts all its local changes. Multiplying each of $r$ columns by a rational scalar $b$ multiplies its top exterior vector by $b^r$. The archimedean logarithm changes by $r log|b|$. At finite places the valuations change by $r v_(p)(b)$ and the weighted sum by $r sum_p v_(p)(b)log p$. The two changes agree by the product formula. For a map with denominators, some local contributions are negative. Choosing different rational multiples for different comparisons can alter each separate local entry even when a global geometric map is the same. A common normalization makes the cancellation visible.

The language of slopes normalizes this accounting by rank. A Euclidean lattice $P$ of rank $r$ has degree $-log vol(P)$ relative to the given metric and slope this degree divided by $r$. For a full sublattice $P'$ of index $N$, its degree is smaller by $log N$. For a saturated inclusion $P_1$ in a larger lattice $P_2$, equip the quotient with the orthogonal quotient metric. Choose bases of $P_1$ and lift a basis of the free quotient. In the Gram-volume formula the second set can be projected off $P_1$ without changing the total volume. Thus
$ op("deg")(P_2)=op("deg")(P_1)+op("deg")(P_2 slash P_1). $
The mean slope is a rank-weighted mean of the slopes of these two pieces. An upper bound on the mean leaves room for a small-rank sublattice of very large slope. The higher-rank argument must control this variation before combining many images.

== Adeles and a unitary group <c05s05>

The finite adeles $𝔸_(F,f)$ of a number field $F$ are tuples in its finite completions which are integral at all but finitely many places. A compact open subgroup imposes a stabilizer at every finite place, with only finitely many exceptional choices. Adjoining the real and complex completions gives the full adeles. In this language simultaneous local conditions can be written as a single quotient.

For $E=ℚ(i)$ with complex conjugation, the group preserving $z overline(z)$ in dimension one is described over $ℚ$ by $a^2+b^2=1$. It is compact at the real place. In higher dimension a positive definite Hermitian form gives the same compactness at infinity. Integral lattices which are isometric locally at every finite place belong to a genus. The genus contains finitely many isometry classes in the definite case. Automorphic forms with fixed weight and level can then be treated as vectors indexed by a finite set of classes with specified stabilizers. The general machinery later supplies both definite and auxiliary indefinite realizations.

== A finite place has its own Fourier transform <c05s06>

Normalize additive Haar measure on $ℚ_p$ so that $ℤ_p$ has volume one. The additive group $p^r ℤ_p$ has volume $p^(-r)$ for every integer $r$, by splitting the unit ball into residue classes and using translation invariance. Choose an additive character $psi$ of $ℚ_p$ that is trivial on $ℤ_p$ and nontrivial on $p^(-1)ℤ_p$. One explicit choice sends the finite negative-power part of the $p$-adic expansion to its usual exponential $exp(2pi i a)$, whose phase is unchanged on adding an integer to the representative. With the corresponding Fourier kernel one obtains
$ integral_(p^r ℤ_p) psi(x y) dif x=p^(-r) bold(1)_(y in p^(-r)ℤ_p). $
If $y$ belongs to the indicated dual ball the integrand is constantly one. Otherwise some $h in p^r ℤ_p$ makes $psi(h y)!=1$. Translating by $h$ leaves the integral unchanged and multiplies it by this nontrivial scalar, forcing it to vanish.

Changing the ball to a coset $h+p^r ℤ_p$ multiplies the answer by $psi(h y)$. Thus coarse support in one variable becomes fine support in the dual, with a volume factor and a phase. This computation is the nonarchimedean analogue of the shifted Gaussian formula. It reduces to a finite Fourier computation. Once a test function is compactly supported and constant on small balls, both its integral and its transform reduce to finite sums. This is what permits conditions on local test functions to be combined with integral lattice conditions.

Additive and multiplicative Haar measures have different elementary normalizations. Normalize $dif^times x$ on $ℚ_p^times$ by giving $ℤ_p^times$ mass one. Every shell $p^n ℤ_p^times$ now also has multiplicative mass one. If an unramified character $chi$ is trivial on $ℤ_p^times$ and $chi(p)=alpha$ with $|alpha p^(-s)|<1$, then
$ integral_(ℚ_p^times) bold(1)_(ℤ_p)(x)chi(x)|x|_p^s dif^times x
 =sum_(n>=0) alpha^n p^(-n s)=frac(1,1-alpha p^(-s)). $
The analytic local Euler factor is already an integral of a characteristic function of an integral lattice. For a character nontrivial on the unit group, using that same test function makes its unit average vanish. The local Euler factor on invariants is then one for a ramified character in dimension one. Representing it by a zeta integral requires a different choice of test function. The vanishing unit average and the missing Euler factor express related facts in different constructions. #link("https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~alexk/2023S572/Tate1950.pdf")[Tate's thesis.]

== Why adeles carry a single rational lattice <c05s07>

A finite adele of $ℚ$ has components $x_p in ℚ_p$ with $x_p in ℤ_p$ at all but finitely many primes. Its integral subgroup is $product_p ℤ_p$. Every finite adele differs by a rational number from this integral subgroup. To prove it, choose a positive denominator $D$ clearing the finitely many exceptional local components. For each $p$ dividing $D$, impose the congruence $m equiv D x_p$ modulo $p^(v_(p)(D))$. The right side has an integral residue and the moduli are relatively prime. The Chinese remainder theorem gives an integer $m$. Then $x_p-frac(m,D) in ℤ_p$ at every place, including the primes that did not divide $D$. Another valid rational number differs from $frac(m,D)$ by an element of $ℚ$ integral at every prime, hence by an integer.

For example, take $x_2=frac(1,4)$, $x_3=frac(1,3)$, and integral components zero elsewhere. One may choose $D=12$, $m equiv 3$ modulo four, and $m equiv 1$ modulo three. The value $m=7$ works. Subtracting $frac(7,12)$ from the component at two leaves $-frac(1,3)$ and from the component at three leaves $-frac(1,4)$. Each is integral at its respective place. There remains one ordinary integer of freedom. It puts the real component into $[0,1)$, so this interval together with the integral finite subgroup gives a fundamental domain for diagonal $ℚ$ in $𝔸_ℚ$, up to boundary. With the above Haar measures the resulting additive quotient has volume one. This calculation is a concrete reason that a single rational vector is the correct object to sum over in an adelic theta series.

Products of local functions make the global construction practical. If a test function equals $bold(1)_(ℤ_p^d)$ at every finite place except finitely many and is a Gaussian at infinity, summing it on $ℚ^d$ reduces to a rapidly convergent sum over rational vectors with bounded denominators. Replacing conditions at finitely many primes by smaller balls restricts the rational vectors to lattice cosets. Changing the finite Fourier transforms changes the dual lattice and the local volume factors exactly as above. The genuinely automorphic theta kernel allows an entire pair of groups to move the test function, but the summation and measure normalizations can already be seen in this restricted product.

== A complete mass calculation for a unitary torus <c05s08>

Let $T$ be the norm-one group for $ℚ(i)$ over $ℚ$. Thus a rational point is an element $z in ℚ(i)^times$ with $z overline(z)=1$. At a split prime its local group is $ℚ_p^times$, represented by pairs $(a,a^(-1))$, while at an inert or ramified prime it is the norm-one group in a quadratic local field. Let $K_p$ consist of the norm-one elements integral together with their inverses in the local algebra and put $K=product_p K_p$. The double-coset spaces $T(ℚ) backslash T(𝔸_f) slash K$ and their refinements can be calculated entirely in Gaussian integers.

At an inert or ramified prime, a local element of norm one has valuation zero. At a split prime choose one of the two prime ideals, with generator $pi_p$ in $ℤ[i]$. The ratio $frac(pi_p,overline(pi_p))$ has valuations $1,-1$ at the two primes above $p$ and zero at all other primes. Only finitely many split primes have nonzero valuations in a given finite idele $g in T(𝔸_f)$. Multiplying $g$ by suitable integer powers of the rational points $frac(pi_p,overline(pi_p))$ thus puts all its components in $K$. Every class in the double quotient is represented by the identity. Its stabilizer consists of rational norm-one elements integral with integral inverse at every prime. These are exactly the four units of $ℤ[i]$.

Now impose level at thirteen, a split prime. Choose the embedding at which $i$ reduces to $5$, a square root of $-1$ there. Let $K'$ be the kernel of the reduction map from the first component of $K_(13)$ to $𝔽_(13)^times$, retaining every other $K_p$. Then $K slash K'$ is cyclic of order twelve. The four global units map injectively to $\{1,-1,5,-5\}$, a subgroup of order four. Because the torus is abelian and the old double-coset space had just one class, the new classes are the orbits of this four-element subgroup on $K slash K'$. There are exactly three. Every stabilizer at the new level is trivial.

Normalize finite Haar measure by $vol(K)=1$. At maximal level the single class contributes $frac(1,4)$ to the volume sum. At the smaller level each class contributes $vol(K')=frac(1,12)$, and the three together again contribute $frac(1,4)$. The number of classes increased, and the integral lattice of functions on the finite set changed its rank. The invariant volume did not change. Counting classes with equal weight one at both levels would have returned one and three. Tracking the stabilizer and the volume of the level subgroup is precisely what reconciles the two computations.

The pullback of scalar functions from the old class set sends a value $a$ to the vector $(a,a,a)$. Give both spaces the mass inner product just computed. Its squared norm is $frac(abs(a)^2,4)$ in the first space and $frac(3abs(a)^2,12)$ in the second, so the pullback is an isometry. The adjoint sends $(b_1,b_2,b_3)$ to $frac(b_1+b_2+b_3,3)$. It can introduce a denominator of three on an integral lattice, despite the isometry in the forward direction over the reals. These exact constants illustrate two simultaneous normalization issues when levels change in the more complicated arithmetic of the proof.

== A period and its integral image <c05s09>

A period is a functional obtained by integrating an automorphic form over a subgroup quotient, possibly against a character or another form. A finite model illustrates the linear algebra. If $K$ is a subgroup of a finite group $G$, the map
$ f mapsto frac(1,abs(K)) sum_(h in K) f(h)overline(lambda(h)) $
picks out a character component on restriction. The factor $frac(1,abs(K))$ controls its operator norm and may introduce a denominator on an integral lattice. Repeated projections can accumulate denominators that are negligible for a single projection. The proof must compare periods from many theta lifts on common integral lattices.

A theta lift uses a kernel, just as an integral transform does. One group acts in the input variable and a second group acts in the output variable. The kernel comes from a common Weil representation. An adjointness calculation identifies two ways to integrate the same function, while norms and integral values can be studied separately. In Chapters 12–14 the lattice, operator norm and dimensions of the common images enter the same determinant inequality.

== Operators behind a theta kernel <c05s10>

Take a vector space of functions on $𝔽_p$, with $p$ odd, and let $e_p$ be the basic additive character. Define translation and modulation by
$ (T_a f)(x)=f(x+a), quad (M_b f)(x)=e_(p)(b x)f(x). $
They obey $T_a M_b=e_(p)(a b)M_b T_a$. The scalar is the commutation relation of the finite Heisenberg group. Normalize the finite Fourier transform by
$ (cal("F")f)(y)=p^(-frac(1,2))sum_(x in 𝔽_p) f(x)e_(p)(-x y). $
Changing variables shows $cal("F")T_a=M_a cal("F")$ and $cal("F")M_b=T_(-b) cal("F")$. Thus Fourier transformation exchanges position and frequency, including the sign needed to preserve the commutation relation. By finite orthogonality it is unitary for the counting inner product.

A second operation is the quadratic phase $U_c f(x)=e_(p)(frac(c x^2,2)) f(x)$, where division by two means multiplication by the inverse of two in $𝔽_p$. Direct expansion of $(x+a)^2$ gives
$ U_c^(-1)T_a U_c=e_(p)(frac(c a^2,2))M_(c a) T_a. $
The action on the position and frequency labels is a linear shear, accompanied by the scalar phase in the formula. Such formulas explain why a representation built from changes of coordinates and Fourier transforms often needs a choice of scalar normalization. Unitarity controls the modulus of that scalar over the complex numbers. Integral valuation and compatibility with the other places require additional information. The finite construction here illustrates the operators used to define a Weil representation without supplying the more specialized local matching theorems used in the proof.

At an archimedean place a polynomial model makes factorials visible. In one complex variable use the Gaussian norm
$ ||P||^2=integral_ℂ |P(z)|^2 e^(-pi |z|^2) dif x dif y, quad z=x+i y. $
Rotation makes the monomials orthogonal. Substituting $u=pi r^2$ in polar coordinates shows $||z^m||^2=frac(m!,pi^m)$. The functional $P mapsto P^((m))(0)$ takes the value $m!$ on $z^m$ and zero on the other monomials. Its norm on the polynomial space with orthogonal monomials of degrees up to any $d>=m$ is therefore $sqrt(m! pi^m)$. Over an integral polynomial ring the same functional takes values divisible by $m!$. A single operation thus has a precise archimedean norm and strong integral divisibility. In several variables the factors multiply over multi-indices. The weights and Frobenius iterates in the actual theta kernel determine which such factors occur and how often.

== A period compared in two orders <c05s11>

For a finite model of an adelic quotient, let a finite group act on a set. Summing a function constant on orbits over all points with normalized counting measure amounts to summing one value per orbit weighted by its size divided by the group order. The weight is therefore the reciprocal of the stabilizer order. For the action of a group of order two on $\{a,b,c\}$ fixing $a$ and exchanging $b,c$, the two weights are $frac(1,2)$ and one. In a definite lattice genus the corresponding stabilizers are automorphism groups with level structure. Their orders enter the measure even when the class set is small.

Let $X,Y$ be finite sets with positive weights $m_x,n_y$. A kernel $K(y,x)$ sends $f$ to $T f(y)=sum_x K(y,x)f(x)m_x$. Take the inner products linear in the first variable. Expanding the pairing and conjugating shows
$ (T^*g)(x)=sum_y overline(K(y,x))g(y)n_y. $
If all input weights were set to one while the output inner product kept its old weights, the displayed adjoint formula would be wrong for the new operator with the old input norm. Comparing integrals in two different orders works because both integrals are pairings with the *same measures*. For a double kernel, taking the $Y$ pairing first or the $X$ pairing first then gives an identity of periods. A seesaw identity in the automorphic argument uses exactly this principle after identifying both kernels as restrictions of a common Weil representation.

For a more arithmetic example let $X$ and $Y$ both have two points of unit weight and take $K=mat(1,1;1,-1)$. Its adjoint equals its transpose and $K^*K=2I$. Rescaling by $frac(1,sqrt(2))$ gives an isometry of real spaces, while over the integers the original matrix has image of index two, consisting of pairs of the same parity. Normalizing solely from the real Hilbert space would hide the finite index. Taking a family of such maps adds a third question, how much of the common target their images jointly occupy. The rank and overlap estimates in the later chapters are what turn a collection of individual divisibility statements into a bound on that common space.

== How overlap controls the dimension of a common image <c05s12>

There is a useful dimension estimate for subspaces made visible by their orthogonal projectors. Let $U_1,dots,U_k$ be subspaces of a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space and $P_i$ their projectors. The nonnegative operator $A=sum_i P_i$ has image the span of the $U_i$. In fact $chevron.l A v,v chevron.r=sum_i ||P_i v||^2$, so its kernel is exactly the common orthogonal complement. The eigenvalues of $A$ are real and nonnegative. If $r$ of them are positive, Cauchy–Schwarz applied to their sum gives $(tr A)^2<=r tr(A^2)$. Write $d_i=dim U_i$. Expanding the traces yields
$ dim(sum_i U_i)>=frac((sum_i d_i)^2,sum_i d_i+sum_(i!=j) tr(P_i P_j)). $
Here $tr(P_i P_j)=tr(P_i P_j P_i)$ is nonnegative, and equals the squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm of the product $P_j P_i$. In orthonormal bases of $U_i$ and $U_j$ it is the sum of the squared absolute values of their scalar products. It can therefore be estimated by trace or matrix-coefficient formulas without directly determining the whole intersection pattern.

For three copies of the same line, $d_i=1$ and every off-diagonal overlap is one. The lower bound gives one, exactly the space occupied. For three orthogonal lines all overlaps vanish and it gives three. For a less extreme example, take three unit vectors with directions at angles $0,120,240$ degrees in a real plane. Each squared scalar product between distinct vectors is $frac(1,4)$. The bound is $frac(9,3+frac(6,4))=2$. A pairwise count of *exact* intersections would have returned zero for all three pairs just as it would for three independent lines in space. The quantitative overlaps detect that a third independent direction is unavailable in the plane.

When the images are columns of maps carrying different scalar normalizations, replacing every image by a projector can conceal the norms of the maps. A closely related bound uses $A=sum_u T_u T_u^*$ and expands its first and second traces. The range is still the span of the images because $chevron.l A v,v chevron.r=sum_u ||T_u^* v||^2$. The diagonal terms now involve singular values of each map, and the mixed terms involve the overlaps of two images *weighted* by their singular values. The inequality $(tr A)^2<=op("rank")(A)tr(A^2)$ remains valid with this choice. This explains why the integral comparison in Part II needs estimates for diagonal errors as well as for overlap between different words of theta lifts.

The later determinant comparison uses all three measurements. Rank counts the dimensions that enter the determinant. The elementary divisors give their finite divisibility, and the real covolume bounds the metric size against which that divisibility is measured.


